# Sampling, Aliasing and Sensor Data Integrity Simulator

## Overview
A Python-based digital signal processing project demonstrating sampling theory, aliasing, anti-alias filtering and missing-sample detection using reproducible synthetic signals.

## Objectives
- Visualize signals sampled at different rates.
- Demonstrate aliasing when the input frequency exceeds the Nyquist limit.
- Simulate anti-alias filtering before sampling.
- Detect missing samples and identify the affected time interval.

## Tools
Python, NumPy, SciPy, pandas, Matplotlib and Jupyter Notebook.

## Experiments

### 1. Sampling a 10 Hz Signal
Compared sampling rates of 20 Hz, 30 Hz and 100 Hz. The experiment illustrates the Nyquist boundary and the benefits of sampling above the minimum theoretical rate.

### 2. Aliasing Demonstration
Sampled a 70 Hz sinusoid at 100 Hz. The expected alias frequency was 30 Hz because the input exceeded the 50 Hz Nyquist frequency.

### 3. Anti-Alias Filtering
Combined a useful 10 Hz sinusoid with a 70 Hz interference component. Simulated a fourth-order Butterworth low-pass filter with a 40 Hz cutoff before downsampling. The filtered samples were closer to the useful waveform.

### 4. Missing-Sample Detection
Generated a ten-second recording at 200 Hz with a deliberate one-second gap. The integrity check identified 200 missing samples between 4 and 5 seconds.

## Key Findings
- Sampling rate determines the Nyquist frequency and affects whether input frequencies can be represented without aliasing.
- Aliased components cannot generally be distinguished from genuine in-band signals using sampled data alone.
- Analog anti-alias filtering must occur before the ADC.
- Missing sample positions can be identified using explicit sample indices.

## Limitations
- All experiments use synthetic data.
- The anti-alias filter is simulated digitally at a higher sampling rate; no physical analog circuit was tested.
- The missing-data experiment uses a deliberately generated gap.
- The results do not establish the performance of a real sensor or ADC.

